course: dictionary
lesson: 10047
title: Terrify and its family
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To terrify someone means to make them extremely frightened. «Thunder terrifies my dog.» The thunder makes the dog very scared, so terrify is a strong word for fear.
explain: Look at the word family. Terrify is the verb. Terrified describes someone who is very, very scared, as in «terrified of spiders». Terrifying describes something that causes great fear, as in a terrifying storm.
explain: Writers choose terrify when they want fear to feel strong and dramatic. Scare is a weaker choice. To check a word, swap it into the sentence and see whether the feeling changes.
fact: Terrify is a verb, which means it is something you do to someone. Its forms are terrifies, terrified and terrifying.
fact: «Terrified» means very, very scared.
fact: «Terrifying» means causing great fear.
fact: People say «terrified of» and «terrified to», as in «terrified of heights».
fact: For ordinary fear, a word like scare is often enough; terrify is the bigger, stronger word.
exercise: «Thunder terrifies my dog.» What does terrifies mean here? | Makes the dog very tired | Makes very happy | *Makes the dog extremely frightened | Makes the dog very quiet
exercise: Which word means causing great fear? | Gentle | Cosy | *Terrifying | Sleepy
exercise: Ben is ___ of heights, so he never climbs ladders. Which word fits best? | Delighted | Terrify | Terrifies | *Terrified
test: «The storm was ___, with lightning cracking over the hills.» Which word fits best? | Tiny | Snoozy | *Terrifying | Cosy
test: Which sentence uses terrify as a verb, to make someone frightened? | The baby ate a biscuit. | The baby is happy and sleepy. | *The loud bang terrifies the baby. | The baby was terrifying the sofa.
exam: Which sentence uses terrified correctly to describe a person? | Sam terrified the dark slowly. | *Sam is terrified of the dark. | The dark terrified of Sam. | Sam terrify the dark tonight.
